TPM event logs OVMF recorded, for td-boot's prediction tests
(src/eventlog_tests.rs; td-install/ENCRYPTION.md "Prediction").

    ovmf-a.log   chain a's log, as Linux presents it in securityfs
    ovmf-b.log   chain b's log
    ovmf.txt     per chain: SHA-256 PCRs 4, 7 and 9 as read in the guest,
                 the kernel file's SHA-256, its Authenticode SHA-256
                 (`application`) and the INITRD's SHA-256
    record.py    the host fixture that recorded them

record.py booted each chain under the host's QEMU 10.2.1 with its bundled
edk2-stable202408 firmware (edk2-x86_64-code.fd, a fresh copy of
edk2-i386-vars.fd, so Secure Boot off), q35, KVM and swtpm 0.10.1 on
tpm-tis, a fresh TPM state per boot, the way the encrypted oracles attach
it; the ESP was a vvfat directory holding EFI/BOOT/BOOTX64.EFI and
EFI/BOOT/INITRD. Its arguments were

    KERNEL         bzImage of linux-x86-64 7.1.4 built from this recipe
                   with CONFIG_SECURITY=y and CONFIG_INTEGRITY off added
                   (td's shipped kernel cannot mount securityfs yet; see
                   "Prediction"), SHA-256 548673b3...caf21f
    SELECTOR_CPIO  a system-x86-64 build's boot/selector-initramfs.cpio

Chain b is chain a with 512 zero bytes after the kernel, which Authenticode
hashes, and 512 after the initramfs, which the kernel's unpacker skips. The
same firmware and variables give the same PCR 7, and a's log with b's
digests substituted predicts b's PCR 4 and PCR 9 exactly.

record.py writes the `application` digests from the logs' one PCR 4
boot-application event each; td-boot's own Authenticode code gives the same digests over the
kernel and its padded copy (the ignored test
`authenticode_agrees_with_ovmf_over_the_recorded_kernel`, run with
TD_BOOT_RECORDED_KERNEL naming that bzImage). The kernel is not committed.

No hardware machine's log is recorded here yet. A hardware log goes beside
these with its own README entry naming the machine and firmware version, and
is never constructed or edited by hand; the refusal cases in the tests are
built from these logs in code instead.
